fixed: ["Title: changed 'Tested Energy per Dollar and per Pound' to 'Measured Energy per Dollar and per Pound'. The rest of the title is unchanged.", "Added row bluetti-elite-100-mini (Elite 100 mini). Price $429 is from storecheck on https://www.bluettipower.com/products/elite-100-mini-portable-power-station (2026-11-15). The same BLUETTI page gives 1,004.8 Wh, 700 W total output, 23.6 lb, the 9.3 × 8.5 in footprint and LFP. Usable 903 Wh was measured by There's A Trick For That, per The Cool Down (Vivian Tran, 2026-09-06), https://www.thecooldown.com/green-tech/bluetti-elite-100-mini-backup-battery-review/. The 97% efficiency is from The Solar Lab, https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/bluetti-elite-100-mini. Arithmetic: 903 ÷ 23.6 = 38.3 Wh/lb; $429 ÷ 903 = $0.48 per usable Wh; 903 ÷ 1,004.8 = 90%.", "Added row bluetti-pioneer-na (Pioneer Na). Price $700 is from storecheck on https://www.bluettipower.com/products/sodium-ion-battery-pioneer-na (2026-11-15). The same BLUETTI page gives 900 Wh, 1,500 W, 2,250 W lifting power and 'just 35 lbs'. I used BLUETTI's 35 lb rather than the brief's 16 kg / 35.3 lb because that is what the maker's page states today. The page also gives charging at -15 °C (5 °F), up to 60% SoC below -10 °C (14 °F), and discharging at -25 °C (-13 °F). The Solar Lab (https://www.thesolarlab.com/review/bluetti-pioneer-na) measured 86% battery efficiency. It reports that the unit charged and discharged down to its rated temperatures, with power throttled by about a t
